# Sibling worktrees (see the global Worktree Convention) — never part of the
# primary checkout's status.
.worktree/

# Go build output. Binaries go to bin/, never the repo root.
bin/
coverage.out

# Self-extractor payloads. A consuming repo's packaging script stages its
# release tree as payload.tar.gz, copies it NEXT TO the installer's main() here,
# and builds with `-tags withpayload` (see go/cmd/*/payload_with.go). The
# artifact is therefore written INTO this checkout by another repo's build, and
# it is tens to hundreds of MB.
#
# voicelab already ignores its own cmd/installer/payload.tar.gz; that line did
# not have an equivalent here, so the first real cutover would leave a large
# untracked binary in this tree, one `git add -A` from being committed.
go/cmd/*/payload.tar.gz

# Dart / Flutter
.dart_tool/
build/
.flutter-plugins-dependencies
# `flutter test --coverage` (make cov) writes here, per package. The kit's own
# .gitignore carries this line and it did not come across with the tranche-0
# copy, so running the gate left an untracked coverage/ tree in the primary
# checkout — which the worktree convention requires to stay pristine.
coverage/
# Local kit/package redirects used while iterating against a worktree; the
# committed pubspec pin is the truth.
pubspec_overrides.yaml

# `go build ./cmd/<name>` writes the binary into the CURRENT directory, not
# into bin/ — so a casual build run from go/ leaves an executable at
# go/<name>, which the bin/ rule above does not cover. Caught exactly that
# way: `go build ./cmd/pin-monitor` during the tools port left go/pin-monitor
# staged by `git add -A`. Ignore the commands by name.
/go/gen-icon
/go/pin-monitor
/go/example-installer

# pin-monitor's config names sibling checkouts on THIS machine by absolute
# path — host-specific by construction, never committed. Copy
# consumers.yaml.example to consumers.yaml and fill in your own paths.
go/cmd/pin-monitor/consumers.yaml

# go.work and go.work.sum are PER-CHECKOUT local state -- one per worktree,
# never committed. go resolves the NEAREST go.work walking upward, so a file at
# the repo root is found from inside every worktree beneath it, where its `use`
# set names the PRIMARY checkout rather than the worktree in hand.
go.work
go.work.sum
